CH6 = {
    'num': '6', 'title': 'CONCLUSION & FUTURE WORK',
    'intro': "This chapter deals with the outcome of the project and its future. It summarises what has been achieved against the objectives set in Chapter 1, states honestly what the work does not show, and proposes enhancements that would take the platform from a complete prototype towards a production system.",
    'sections': [
        ('6.1', 'CONCLUSION', [
            ('p', "The project has produced a working platform that monitors the health and the security of IoT devices, predicts imminent breaches of alert limits with an explanation, and answers maintenance questions from live data and from the organisation's own documents. All the objectives stated in Chapter 1 were met: devices of different machine types are monitored with their own limits; readings are stored and analysed as they arrive; alerts, incidents, firmware status and maintenance history are available in one place and are pushed to the dashboard; every prediction is explained with exact feature contributions; models are retrained from telemetry, evaluated on newer data, versioned and protected by a validation gate; documents are uploaded, validated and indexed; and the assistant gives grounded answers with verifiable citations while being unable to change anything."),
            ('p', "The design shows that a language model can be added to an operational system responsibly. The model is only the last step of a pipeline whose earlier steps are deterministic: a planner selects fixed read-only queries, the retrieval applies the user's access rights inside the database query, the text of documents is treated as data, and the answer is checked before it is stored. Running the model locally removes the need to send maintenance documents to an external service. Splitting the data between MongoDB (operational documents and time-stamped readings), PostgreSQL with pgvector (vectors, full-text search and access control in one query) and Redis (hot state and messaging) gave each kind of data a suitable home, and the migration tool shows that an installation can be moved between stores without loss."),
            ('p', "The quality of the result is supported by evidence: 176 backend tests, 39 front-end tests and a thirteen-step browser test passed, the type checker and the linter report nothing, the whole stack starts from a single command, and several real defects were found and corrected through testing. At the same time the work has limits that should be stated plainly. The accuracy of the supplied model was measured on synthetic data and therefore does not show how it would perform on real machines; the labels mean that a threshold was crossed, not that equipment failed; the assistant was verified with a small local model, and the best default model needs more graphics memory than the development laptop has; the continuous-integration workflow had not yet run on the hosting service when this report was written; and the system has not been tested with a large fleet of real devices or under heavy load."),
        ]),
        ('6.2', 'FUTURE ENHANCEMENTS', [
            ('p', "Several improvements would increase the value and the robustness of the platform. The most important is **validation with real devices**: connecting physical equipment, collecting a period of genuine data, labelling real failures, retraining the models and measuring their accuracy on real behaviour. This would also make it possible to compare the logistic-regression model with other models, such as gradient-boosted trees or sequence models, while keeping the explanation requirement."),
            ('p', "On the device side, gateways for the protocols that are common in industry (MQTT, OPC UA and CoAP) can be added in front of the REST API, together with signed firmware images and a real over-the-air update mechanism instead of the simulated roll-out. On the security side, HTTPS termination with certificate management, single sign-on, fine-grained permissions in addition to the two roles, secret rotation, and e-mail, SMS or messaging-application notifications would be needed in production."),
            ('p', "For the assistant, optical character recognition would allow scanned manuals to be indexed; a formal evaluation set of questions with known answers would allow the retrieval settings and the choice of models to be tuned and compared objectively; larger or fine-tuned models could be used where hardware permits; multilingual documents and questions could be supported; and an approval workflow could let the assistant propose actions (for example creating a maintenance entry) that a person confirms, while keeping the rule that the assistant itself never writes."),
            ('p', "On the data side, a MongoDB replica set would enable multi-document transactions and high availability, long-term aggregated telemetry would allow trends over months while the raw readings expire, and monitoring of the models for drift would signal when retraining is due. Finally, container orchestration, centralised logging and a mobile application for engineers in the field would suit larger deployments."),
        ]),
    ],
}
